course: everyday
lesson: 41
title: Crossing the road
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A car needs time and distance to stop, and its driver may not see a small child, so before crossing you always stop at the kerb, look and listen until the road is clear.
explain: The safe routine is: find a safe place to cross, stop at the edge, look both ways and listen, wait until no traffic is coming, then walk straight across, still looking and listening.
explain: Which way you look first depends on the side the traffic drives on: you look first towards the lane nearest to you, because those cars reach you first.
explain: In the USA and Canada cars drive on the right, so the nearest cars come from your left; in the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand cars drive on the left, so the nearest cars come from your right.
explain: A boy from Ohio visiting London who looks left first, as he does at home, may step out right in front of a bus coming from his right; that is why London paints LOOK RIGHT on the road at many crossings.
explain: A boy in Ohio ran into the road after his ball without stopping; a car could not stop in time and hit him, and he spent weeks in hospital with a broken leg.
explain: A girl in Manchester stepped out from between two parked vans; the driver coming along could not see her until the last second, and only just managed to swerve.
fact: A moving car cannot stop at once; the faster it goes, the longer it takes to stop.
fact: Before crossing you stop at the kerb, look both ways and listen for traffic.
fact: You cross only when no traffic is coming, and you keep looking and listening while you cross.
fact: You walk straight across the road, never run, because running makes you more likely to trip and fall, and never cross diagonally, on a slant, which keeps you in the road longer.
fact: In the USA and Canada traffic drives on the right, so you look left first.
fact: In the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand traffic drives on the left, so you look right first.
fact: Headphones and phones take your attention and hearing away from the road.
fact: Quiet electric cars can be very hard to hear, so your eyes matter as much as your ears.
fact: Young children should cross roads holding an adult's hand.
fact: A bend or the top of a hill hides cars from you, and hides you from drivers, so it is a bad place to cross.
fact: In the UK the steps of road crossing are taught as the Green Cross Code.
fact: A car going at 30 miles per hour needs about 23 metres to stop, which is about five car lengths.
fact: A child hit by a car is much more likely to be badly hurt than an adult, because the car hits the head and body.
exercise: What is the very first thing you do when you reach the edge of a road you want to cross? | Stop at the kerb before stepping out | Step out a little way so you can see the cars better | Start walking and look as you go | Run quickly while the road is empty
exercise: A boy in Texas is about to cross. Which way should he look first? | To his left, because cars drive on the right | To his right, because cars drive on the left | Straight ahead, across the road | Up, to check the traffic light only
exercise: A girl in Leeds is about to cross. Which way should she look first? | To her right, because cars drive on the left | To her left, because cars drive on the right | Behind her, for bicycles | Only at the cars that are parked
exercise: Why do you look first towards the lane nearest to you? | The cars in that lane will reach you first | Drivers in that lane always drive faster | The far lane is always empty | It is the law to look only one way
exercise: In which of these countries does traffic drive on the left? | Australia | Canada | The USA | None of them
exercise: In which of these countries does traffic drive on the right? | Canada | New Zealand | Ireland | The UK
exercise: Why should you keep looking and listening while you cross? | A car can appear or turn in while you cross | Once you start, cars must always stop for you | It helps you walk in a straight line | It is only needed on very wide roads
exercise: Why is it safer to walk across a road than to run? | Running makes you more likely to trip and fall | Drivers do not notice slow walkers | Walking makes cars slow down by law | Running is only unsafe in the rain
exercise: Why is it not safe to cross with headphones playing loud music? | You may not hear a car coming | Music makes the drivers angry with you | Headphones make you walk slower | The road noise can hurt your ears
exercise: Why is crossing just past a sharp bend in the road dangerous? | Drivers coming round the bend cannot see you in time | Cars always speed up out of a bend | The road is wider at a bend | Bends are where the road is most slippery
exercise: Why are electric cars a special danger to people crossing? | They can be so quiet you do not hear them | They cannot use their brakes well | They always drive much faster than other cars | Their lights are harder to see
exercise: A fast car and a slow car both brake at the same moment. Which stops in the shorter distance? | The slow car | The fast car, since it brakes harder | They stop in the same distance | Whichever car is bigger
exercise: What is the safe way for a five-year-old to cross a busy road? | Holding an adult's hand | Running ahead of the adult | Crossing with older friends | Waving at the cars first
exercise: Where do you wait while you look and listen before crossing? | On the pavement or sidewalk, just back from the kerb | Halfway into the road between parked cars | On the white line in the middle | Anywhere, as long as you look carefully
test: A family from Florida is on holiday in Sydney. What must the children change when they cross roads there? | Look right first instead of left | Look left first as at home | Look only straight ahead | Nothing, the rule is the same everywhere
test: A boy in Dublin looks left, sees nothing, and steps out without looking right. What could happen? | A car in the nearest lane, coming from his right, could hit him | Nothing, the nearest cars come from his left | Cars from the right always stop for him | Only bicycles come from the right in Dublin
test: Which is the safest place for a child to cross a road? | A straight stretch where drivers can see you from far away | Just after a bend, where the road is quiet and few cars come | At the top of a hill, where you can see far | Between two parked vans, where it is short
test: A girl hears no cars, so she crosses without looking. Why is this a mistake? | A quiet electric car or a bicycle might be coming | Cars always honk before they get close | Hearing is always better than looking | Cars cannot drive without making a loud noise
test: Which is true about stopping a car? | A faster car needs more distance to stop | A car can always stop at once if the driver brakes hard | Big cars stop sooner than small cars | Stopping distance is the same at every speed
test: A boy is in the middle of the road when a car turns into the street. What should he do? | Keep walking straight across, without running | Turn round and run back as fast as he can to where he started | Stop and wait in the middle of the road | Run diagonally to the nearest side
test: In which country would you look left first before crossing? | Canada | New Zealand | The UK | Australia
test: Why does a crossing in London have the words LOOK RIGHT painted on the road? | Visitors from right-driving countries tend to look left first | London traffic comes only from the right | It means pedestrians must walk to the right | It tells drivers which way to turn
test: Why does a child need to be extra careful near roads compared with an adult? | Children are smaller and harder for drivers to see | Cars may drive faster when children are near | Children are not allowed to cross roads | Drivers only look for adults
test: just run across, no cars rn | Walk straight across, don't run | Yes, run diagonally across it | Run with your eyes on your phone | Run while the cars are slowing down
test: cross at the bend, it's quicker | No, cars can't see you there | Yes, bends are quiet, nobody drives there | Yes, if you're quick | Yes, at the top of the hill
test: car's far away, it'll stop if i go | No, cars can't stop at once | Yes, cars stop instantly if they see you | Yes, if you wave at the driver | Yes, if it's driving slowly enough
test: little bro crossing with me, what do i do | Hold his hand | Let him run ahead | Let him cross alone | Push him across fast
test: ball rolled into the road, quick before it goes far | Stop at the kerb, let it go or ask an adult | Run after it fast before it rolls too far away | Run if no car is close | Dash out then look
test: crossing between the parked vans is quicker | Find a clear place where drivers can see you | Cross between them, it is quicker and shorter | Run so you are seen faster | Cross with your hood up
test: Tom says cars can stop straight away if they see u. right? | No, cars need many metres to stop | Yes, modern brakes stop a car at once | Yes, if the driver is quick | Yes, unless it is raining
test: did u hear a girl got hit crossing on the bend by the school cos the car couldn't see her | Real lesson: never cross on a bend; cross where drivers can see you | Real lesson: cars should never be allowed to drive anywhere near schools | It proves only girls get hit by cars | It is gossip, so bends are fine to cross on
test: my cousin from England says u always look right first, so that's the rule everywhere | Not everywhere; where cars drive on the right, as in the USA, you look left first | He is right; it is the rule in every country | He is right, unless it is raining | He is right, look right only in cities
test: Leo ran across the road and a car honked and he tripped, people keep sharing it | Do not share it; he could have been hurt and it would embarrass him | Share it, it is funny because he tripped and everyone saw him fall over | Share it, so he learns his lesson | Share it, and add that he cried
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the stopping of a moving car? | A car cannot stop at once, and the greater its speed the longer it requires to stop | A car stops instantly whenever its brakes are applied | Speed makes no difference whatsoever to the distance which a moving car requires to stop | Faster cars require less distance to stop
exam: It being given that a pedestrian in the United Kingdom is about to cross, identify the direction in which he ought first to look. | To the right, traffic proceeding upon the left | To the left, traffic proceeding upon the right | Behind, traffic approaching from the rear | Upward, towards the traffic lights only
exam: Identify the correct manner of crossing a road. | Walking straight across without running, whilst continuing to look and listen | Running diagonally so as to minimise the time spent in the road by the pedestrian | Walking slowly whilst reading a phone | Crossing between moving cars when there is a gap
exam: Select the response that is consistent with established guidance concerning young children crossing roads. | Young children should cross holding the hand of an adult | Young children should cross alone in order to learn independence | Young children should cross only at a run | Young children should cross only when it is dark
